// Mariana OVERRIDE — snobol4ever.  Sublime merges every .sublime-color-scheme
// sharing a name, with Packages/User winning, so this file adds rules to the
// stock Mariana without forking it.  Install: copy to Packages/User/.
//
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS.  SPITBOL keywords (&TRIM, &ANCHOR, &ALPHABET, &LCASE)
// are built-in constants and carry the scopes that say so — constant.language
// for the protected ones, constant.other for the settable ones.  Stock Mariana
// paints those red-italic and pink; we want them yellow-orange, the colour it
// reserves for literals, because a keyword reads as a constant.  Retagging them
// in the syntax file as constant.numeric would buy the colour by lying about
// what they are and would follow us into every other colour scheme.  A scheme
// override buys the same colour, changes nothing about the language model, and
// is undone by deleting this file.
//
// hsl(32, 93%, 66%) is Mariana's own "orange" variable, spelled literally so
// this file does not depend on variable inheritance from the parent scheme.
{
    "name": "Mariana",
    "rules":
    [
        {
            // ⭐ THE LABEL CYAN (s182).  Statement labels and their goto targets now carry
            // entity.name.label.sno, NOT entity.name.function.sno.  Mariana EXCLUDES
            // entity.name.label from its orange rule --
            //     "entity.name - (entity.name.section | entity.name.tag | entity.name.label)"
            // -- and styles it nowhere else, so stock leaves it unstyled (white) and this rule
            // owns it outright.  Nothing else in any language competes for it, unlike blue5,
            // which stock also spends on punctuation, bracket contents, mapping keys and
            // annotations.  Cost of the move, stated plainly: without this file the labels are
            // plain white instead of inheriting blue5.
            //
            // WHY CYAN AND NOT THE OLD TEAL.  Mariana has exactly ONE colour at cyan's hue --
            // blue5 = hsl(180, 36%, 54%) -- so hue 180 was always the right hue; what read as
            // "teal" was the SATURATION.  The previous override lifted lightness 54% -> 70% but
            // left saturation at 42%, and high-lightness/low-saturation is what washes a hue out.
            // Raising saturation instead gives cyan without the glare of orange (hsl 32, 93%).
            // To taste, edit the SATURATION: 42% = old pale teal, 58% = this, 68% = vivid cyan.
            // Keep lightness at or below ~66% or it washes out again.
            "name": "SNOBOL4 statement label + goto target — cyan",
            "scope": "entity.name.label.sno",
            "foreground": "hsl(180, 58%, 62%)"
        },
        {
            // NOW ONLY the DEFINE('FOO()') name and interpolated-string variables — the labels
            // moved to entity.name.label.sno above.  Kept at the lifted teal so DEFINE names do
            // not jump; delete this rule to return them to stock blue5.
            // THE LABEL TEAL, ONE SHADE UP.  Stock Mariana gives entity.name.function
            // its "blue5" = hsl(180, 36%, 54%), and spends that same variable on
            // punctuation, bracket contents, string keys and the indent guides — so
            // lightening the VARIABLE would move all of those, in every language.
            // Scoping the lift to entity.name.function.sno lifts only the SNOBOL4
            // labels, their goto targets, and the name inside DEFINE('FOO()').
            // To taste, edit the one lightness number: 54% = stock, 60% = subtle,
            // 54% = stock, 60% = subtle, 64% = previous, 70% = current, 76% = very pale.
            "name": "SNOBOL4 label / goto target / DEFINE name — lighter teal",
            "scope": "entity.name.function.sno",
            "foreground": "hsl(180, 42%, 70%)"
        },
        {
            "name": "SNOBOL4 keyword — &TRIM, &ANCHOR, &ALPHABET, &LCASE, &STCOUNT ...",
            "scope": "constant.language.sno, constant.other.sno, punctuation.definition.keyword.begin.sno, punctuation.definition.variable.begin.sno",
            "foreground": "hsl(32, 93%, 66%)",
            "font_style": ""
        }
    ]
}
